Transform the provided drug JSON data using these exact rules.

Input assumptions
- Input can be either:
  - an array of drug objects, or
  - an object containing `drugs` as an array.
- Each drug may contain:
  - `pharmaceutics` as either a single object or an object map keyed by any id.
  - `drugComponents` as either an object map keyed by any id, or an array of objects.

Required output shape
Return ONLY valid JSON in this exact structure:

{
  "drugs": [],
  "pharmaceuticsIndex": {},
  "drugComponentIndex": {}
}

Transformation rules
1. Build `pharmaceuticsIndex`
- Extract every pharmacology object from every drug.
- Use `pharmacology.code` as the key.
- Use the full pharmacology object as the value.
- If the same code appears again, keep only one entry (no duplicates).

2. Build `drugComponentIndex`
- Extract every component from every drug.
- Use `component.name` as the key.
- Use `{ "name": component.name }` as the value.
- If the same component name appears again, keep only one entry (no duplicates).

3. Transform each drug object
- Remove `pharmaceutics`.
- Add `pharmaceuticsCode` as an array of pharmacology codes found for that drug.
- Remove `drugComponents`.
- Add `drugComponentNames` as an array of component names found for that drug.

4. Normalization and safety
- Preserve all other drug fields unchanged.
- Skip invalid pharmacology entries missing `code`.
- Skip invalid component entries missing `name`.
- Ensure arrays (`pharmaceuticsCode`, `drugComponentNames`) contain unique values per drug.

Example input
{
  "code": "GEN-NDF-0001",
  "name": "Abacavir",
  "drugComponents": [
   { "name": "Abacavir" }
  ],
  "pharmaceutics": {
    "code": "PHARM-NDF-0001",
    "clinicalName": "Abacavir"
  }
}

Example transformed drug (inside `drugs`)
{
  "code": "GEN-NDF-0001",
  "name": "Abacavir",
  "pharmaceuticsCode": ["PHARM-NDF-0001"],
  "drugComponentNames": ["Abacavir"]
}


